 # Ray-Direction Child Ordering
 
-Also called: Direction-Aware Traversal Order, Front-to-Back Child Visit Order, Ordered Octree Traversal, Sequential Octree Traversal
+Also called: Direction-Aware Traversal Order, Front-to-Back Child Visit Order, Front-to-Back Octree Traversal, Front-to-Back Octree Traversal for Ray Casting, Lookup Table for Ray Direction Ordering, Ordered Octree Traversal, Sequential Octree Traversal
 
 ## Overview
-Ray-Direction Child Ordering is an iterative traversal optimization for sparse voxel octrees that processes child nodes in a specific sequence determined by the ray's direction, ensuring that nodes closer to the ray origin are visited first [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the inefficiency of standard recursive traversal, which examines all eight children and performs redundant intersection tests for non-intersected subtrees, by using a deterministic neighbor-linking strategy to skip empty or non-intersected branches [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, it avoids recursion entirely within a node's children by maintaining a `currNode` state and computing the next neighbor based on exit plane t-values, thereby reducing the number of intersection tests and improving performance on sparse structures [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Ray-Direction Child Ordering is an iterative traversal optimization for sparse voxel octrees that processes child nodes in a sequence determined by the ray's direction, ensuring nodes closer to the ray origin are visited first [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the inefficiency of standard recursive traversal, which examines all eight children and performs redundant intersection tests for non-intersected subtrees, by using a deterministic neighbor-linking strategy to skip empty or non-intersected branches [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, it avoids recursion entirely within a node's children by maintaining a `currNode` state and computing the next neighbor based on exit plane t-values, thereby reducing the number of intersection tests and improving performance on sparse structures [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
